      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259236",
          "text": "<p>A graph is called hamiltonian if it has hamiltonian cycle.</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$G$ is Eulerian Hamiltonian but not bipaartite</li>\n    <li>$G$ is Hamiltonian Eulerian but not bipaartite</li>\n    <li>$G$ is neither&nbsp;Eulerian&nbsp;nor Hamiltonian</li>\n    <li>$G$ is Hamiltonian but not Eulerian</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259235",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following integer sequence if used as degree sequence would represent the tree?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n    <li>1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 4</li>\n    <li>1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2</li>\n    <li>1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only I</li>\n    <li>Only II</li>\n    <li>Only I and III</li>\n    <li>Only II and III</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259234",
          "text": "<p>A simple graph $G$ has $20$ vertices. Any two distinct vertices $u$ and $v$ are such that&nbsp;&nbsp;$ deg(u) + deg(v) \\geq 19.$ Which of the following statements is FALSE?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$G$ must have a cycle</li>\n    <li>$G$ can be a disconnected graph</li>\n    <li>$G$ may not have a bridge</li>\n    <li>$G$ may not have a cut-vertex\n    <p>&nbsp;</p>\n    </li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,graph-connectivity",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259233",
          "text": "The edge graph of a graph $G$ is the graph with vertex set $E(G)$ in which two vertices are joined iff they are adjacent edges in $G.$ The no. of edges in the edge graph of complete graph $ K_{10} $ is _____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "360",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,numerical-answers,graph-connectivity",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259232",
          "text": "<p>How many paths of length $4$ are there in complete graph $K_7$?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$1260$</li>\n    <li>$420$</li>\n    <li>$35$</li>\n    <li>$210$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259231",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following statements is false?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>if G(V,E) is a simple graph and&nbsp;$ \\delta \\geq K $, then G has a simple path of length K</li>\n    <li>if G(V,E) is a simple graph and $ |E| &gt; \\binom{|V|-1}{2} $&nbsp;then G is connected</li>\n    <li>If G is a simple graph and&nbsp;$ \\delta &gt; [\\frac{V}{2}]-1 $, then G is connected</li>\n    <li>There can be two vertex disjoint longest paths in connected graph</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259230",
          "text": "<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>A sequence $d=(d1, d2, d3, \\dots, dn)$ is <strong>graphic </strong>if there is a simple graph with sequence $d$.</p>\n\n<p>S1: Sequence $(7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 3, 2)$ is graphic</p>\n\n<p>S2: Sequence $(6, 6, 5, 4, 3, 3, 1)$&nbsp;is graphic</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only S1 is true</li>\n    <li>Only S2 is true</li>\n    <li>Both S1 and S2 are true</li>\n    <li>Neither S1 nor&nbsp;S2 are true</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259229",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\delta$ be the smallest degree of a vertex of $G$.</p>\n\n<p>S1: If $2$ distinct circuits of a graph $G$ contain an edge $e$, then there exists a circuit of $G$ not containing $e$</p>\n\n<p>S2: Let $\\delta$ be the smallest degree of a vertex of $G$. If $\\delta \\geq 2$, then $G$ contains a cycle</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only S1</li>\n    <li>Only S2</li>\n    <li>Both S1 and S2</li>\n    <li>None of the above</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259228",
          "text": "<p>Let $G$ be a graph containing a cycle $v1, \\dots , vk$. If there is an edge $e$ in this cycle with weight strictly larger than all other edges. Which of the following statements is always true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>There exist at least $2$ minimum spanning trees</li>\n    <li>$e \\notin E(T)$ for any minimum weight spanning tree $T$</li>\n    <li>$e \\in E(T)$ for some minimum weight spanning $T$</li>\n    <li>There is a unique minimum spanning tree for the graph $G$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259227",
          "text": "<p>For which of the following does there exist a graph $G = (V, E)$&nbsp; satisfying the specified conditions ?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>A tree with $9$ vertices and the sum of the degrees of all the vertices is $18$ .</li>\n    <li>A graph with $5$ components, $12$ vertices and $7$ edges.</li>\n    <li>A graph with $5$ components, $30$ vertices and $24$ edges.</li>\n    <li>A graph with $9$ vertices, $9$ edges, and no cycles.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259226",
          "text": "<p>The number of directed simple graphs with labelled&nbsp;vertices with $\\mid V \\mid =3$ is</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$27$</li>\n    <li>$64$</li>\n    <li>$21$</li>\n    <li>$48$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259225",
          "text": "The largest possible number of vertices in a graph with $19$ edges and all vertices of degree at least $3$ is _______",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "12",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,numerical-answers,graph-connectivity",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259224",
          "text": "The line graph $L(G)$ of a simple graph $G$ is the graph whose vertices are in one-to-one correspondence with edges of $G,$ two vertices of $L(G)$ are adjacent if and only if the corresponding edges of $G$ are adjacent. If $G$ is regular graph with degree $10$ then $L(G)$ is regular with degree _____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "18",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,numerical-answers",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259223",
          "text": "<p>A certain tree of order $n$ has only vertices of degree $3$ and degree $1$. How many degree $3$ vertices does the tree have?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$ \\frac{n-1}{2} $</li>\n    <li>$ \\frac{n-2}{2} $</li>\n    <li>$ \\frac{n}{2} $</li>\n    <li>$ \\frac{n-1}{3} $</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259222",
          "text": "<p>A graph is&nbsp;<em>critical</em>&nbsp;if the removal of any one of its vertices (and the edges adjacent to that vertex) results in a graph with a lower chromatic number. Which of the following graph is k-critical graph?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n    <li>$ K_5 $</li>\n    <li>$ K_{3,4} $</li>\n    <li>Cycle graph with 4 vertices</li>\n</ol>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only I</li>\n    <li>Only I and II</li>\n    <li>Only I and III</li>\n    <li>All of I, II and III</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259221",
          "text": "Maximum number of edges a graph with $10$ vertices and $4$ components can have is",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "21",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,numerical-answers,graph-connectivity",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259220",
          "text": "$G(V,E)$ is a simple graph with $8$ vertices. The edges of $G$ are decided by tossing the coin for each $2$ vertices combination. Edge is added between any two vertices iff head is turned up. Expected number of edges in the Graph $G(V,E)$ is _____",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "14",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,numerical-answers",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259219",
          "text": "How many different colorings are possible for a cycle graph on three vertices with $5$ colors?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "60",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5,numerical-answers,graph-coloring",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259218",
          "text": "<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>S1: A tree with $n$ vertices which has no vertex of degree $2$ must have at least $(n+2)/2$ leaves.</p>\n\n<p>S2: The no of trees on $5$ labelled vertices is $125$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only S1</li>\n    <li>Only S2</li>\n    <li>Only S1 and S2</li>\n    <li>None of the above</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259217",
          "text": "<p>The size of a Maximum independent set of the complete&nbsp;bipartite graph&nbsp;$ K_{m,n} (m&gt;0,n&gt;0)$ is</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$\\text{max} (m,n)$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{min}(m,n)$</li>\n    <li>$2$</li>\n    <li>$ \\left \\lceil (m+n)/2 \\right \\rceil $</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm5",
          "category": "Graph Theory"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}